1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,870
In sixteen seventy-one, Giovanni Cassini
discovered a new moon of Saturn.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,330
Then, it vanished. On one side
of Saturn, it shone clearly.

3
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:13,650
On the other, it was gone.

4
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:20,260
Cassini guessed why. One half of
this moon must be bright as snow,

5
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:24,030
and the other almost black. He was right.

6
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:28,550
But it took three centuries to learn
just how strange this place really is.

7
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:33,790
Because the two faces aren't
the strangest thing about it.

8
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:39,270
This moon also has a wall: a mountain
range over ten kilometres high,

9
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:42,010
running almost perfectly
around its equator.

10
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:43,970
And nobody knows why.

11
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:49,210
This is Iapetus, Saturn's two-faced moon.

12
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:54,160
It's about fourteen hundred and seventy
kilometres across, roughly the distance

13
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:59,490
from London to Rome. Today, we're going
there to try and solve its mysteries.

14
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:05,430
Iapetus lives far out, about three and
a half million kilometres from Saturn.

15
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:10,130
One orbit takes seventy-nine days,
and because it's tidally locked,

16
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:13,530
a single day here lasts
seventy-nine days too.

17
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:18,750
It's barely denser than
water, so it's mostly ice.

18
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:24,440
And its orbit is tilted, so from its
surface you'd see Saturn's rings opened out

19
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:27,340
across the sky, not edge-on.

20
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,030
That would be quite a view.

21
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:34,750
Its trailing side reflects more than
half the sunlight that hits it.

22
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:40,220
But its leading side, the side facing
forward in its orbit, reflects only about

23
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:44,030
four percent. That's as
dark as fresh asphalt.

24
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:51,780
Arthur C. Clarke's novel, 2001: A Space
Odyssey, imagined that bright patch as

25
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:54,610
artificial, a signpost left for us.

26
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:57,030
The truth turned out to be stranger.

27
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:04,730
In two thousand and four, the
Cassini spacecraft reached Saturn.

28
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,890
Up close, Iapetus's dark coat proved thin.

29
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:14,530
Small, young craters had punched right
through it, to bright ice below.

30
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,750
So where did the dark coat come from?

31
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:21,590
There's one big clue. The dark
side is the leading side.

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,190
Think of driving through
a swarm of insects.

33
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:26,550
Only the windscreen gets splattered.

34
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:36,350
Far beyond Iapetus, a small, dark moon
called Phoebe orbits Saturn backwards.

35
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:42,330
In two thousand and nine, a telescope
found a giant ring of dust coming from it.

36
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:48,190
It's the largest known
ring around any planet.

37
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:51,570
Around a billion Earths
could fit inside it.

38
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:55,890
That dust spirals inward, and
because it's moving backwards,

39
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:57,490
it hits Iapetus head-on.

40
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:05,360
But dust alone would leave a soft
smudge, and Iapetus has sharp,

41
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:09,310
stark edges. Something is
amplifying the effect.

42
00:03:09,420 --> 00:03:13,290
And the answer, it seems, comes
from that incredibly long day.

43
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:19,610
Dark patches absorb more sunlight,
so they warm up slightly.

44
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:24,170
That's enough for the ice there
to turn into vapour, drift away,

45
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:27,290
and freeze again on
colder, brighter ground.

46
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:34,710
So dark areas get darker, and
bright areas get brighter.

47
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:38,460
It's a runaway loop that may
have run for billions of years.

48
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:43,210
Clarke's signpost was painted
by dust, and by the Sun.

49
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:51,300
Now, that wall. The main ridge stretches
for around thirteen hundred kilometres.

50
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:54,990
In places, it rises about
twenty kilometres high.

51
00:03:55,420 --> 00:03:58,010
That's more than twice the
height of Mount Everest.

52
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:06,290
Iapetus is also squashed, shaped like a
moon spinning once every sixteen hours.

53
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,370
Yet today, it spins once
every seventy-nine days.

54
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:14,470
Its shape seems to have frozen
in place when it was young.

55
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,290
One idea is that the crust
buckled as its spin slowed.

56
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:24,180
Another is more dramatic: that
Iapetus once had a ring of its own,

57
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,690
which slowly fell, raining
debris along its equator.

58
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:32,250
No one knows which is right.

59
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:37,130
Cassini ended its mission in twenty
seventeen, plunging into Saturn,

60
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,790
and no spacecraft is planned to return.

61
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:43,230
For now, Iapetus keeps its secrets.

62
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:50,210
Three and a half centuries ago, one man
noticed a point of light that disappeared.

63
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:53,290
What we found there was
stranger than fiction.

64
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:55,460
Which moon should we visit next?

65
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:57,100
Let me know below.
